ood at  everything and that is perfectly okay  and once we realize this we then know  that the sky is not the limit and we  won't be overwhelmed with possibilities  instead you can focus on what's  realistic for you  allowing yourself to focus on the skills  that you have instead of trying to reach  unattainable goals that will only  frustrate you so my own limitations and  workarounds for these limitations when i  was figuring out my music video were  limitation one i could not model and i  still can't model so my work around was  to work with concept artist braden may  who can model  and also to use a couple of models from  the unreal engine marketplace limitation  number two the concept artist had  limited time to work on this project  and 3d modeling was not their primary  strength so the workaround for this was  to work together to limit the amount and  complexity of the 3d models so that the  workload was manageable and attainable  for the concept artist limitation number  three is that i have no 3d animation  ability whatsoever so the workaround was  that i would have to look for an  alternative to bring movement to the  scene using my other skills which is  more of a tech designer more on that  later and limitation number four is that  i do have a tendency to strive for  perfection even though there's no such  thing so the workaround for this was  that i gave myself a soft deadline of  two months to complete this project uh  just so that i had a date to hold myself  accountable for  two four two four when i knew my  limitations and possible workarounds for  them i could then put together a plan  for the music video itself i decided to  have the music video focus on a really  simple bedroom having the camera pan  around it and showing elements of my  real room so it had a personal touch to  it and then with a more solid plan in  mind i could then create a list of what  i needed to bring that to fruition what  i needed was  one low potty 3d bedroom and  individual assets for that bedroom each  of these assets would have one material  per object and these would be created by  my lovely concept artist braden i also  made sure that the concept artist knew  that the walls and the objects of the  room needed to be their own separate  models so that i could hide or show them  to allow free movement of the camera  without having to worry about it  clipping through any of the environment  i needed low poly 3d assets from the  unreal engine marketplace to create a  city view from the bedroom window i  needed to have some sprite animations  created to add some movement into the  video and finally include elements of my  real bedroom as textures within the 3d  ones such as pages for my journal or  photos around my bedroom of me and my  friends  [Music]  documentation i know i know nobody wants  to document when they get to this stage  in the process they just want to get the  ball rolling that's the fun part right  but a solid piece of documentation is  vital to make a cohesive creation and to  stop you falling into the trap of  feature creep  because that can make you work on  something forever and then suddenly it  becomes something completely different  you don't want to fall into that hole  not all the time sometimes it can be  good but not all but not all the time  anyway this is beneficial for both the  cohesivity of the project and also your  own mental health and if you're working  with others it also just really helps to  clarify your vision to make sure that  everybody's on the same wavelength so my  documentation detailed the music video  narrative that i needed  the color palette that i would reference  when creating my materials the reference  images for the 3d models that had  inspired my vision a detailed list of  the 3d models and assets that i needed  from my concept artist links that would  be useful for me during the development  phase tutorials things like that and a  to-do list for the development process  so that i could take things off as i  went along and i knew that i wasn't  missing anything step two preparing art  for a digital environment  so 3d modelling this image is the  wonderful 3d room that concept artist  brayden may created for me in blender  thank you very much it's very cute very  good indeed these assets had a very low  poly count and also most of them had no  texture map which made the artist's job  much faster however it did mean that i  couldn't bake the lights as the texture  information was completely wrong on most  of the models and it made the shadows  extremely odd however this is fine  because i was going to be working with  mainly dynamic lighting as the scene was  extremely low poly and very performant  to work with and i wasn't making a game  i was making a cinematic so it's fine  just so you know dynamic lighting is  when all of the lights are running in  real time and baked lighting is when the  lights are baked into textures hence why  the latter is much more performant and  you should probably do that if you're  making a game or a much much bigger  project within a game engine i'll go  more into depth on the lighting in the  development part of this video once i  put these models into my level and  unreal engine i could then just move the  assets around the room where i wanted  them to be and i created a new material  for each of the objects so that i could  have it match the color palette that i  had in my documentation  including  the musician  so a very common trope of music videos  is to include the musician or musicians  in the video either playing the song and  the instruments or just being a part of  the song's narrative i knew that i also  wanted to include myself into the music  video but i wasn't quite sure how for  quite a long time i don't have the  technology or skills to  3d model myself or 3d animate  so i actually ended up recycling an idea  that i came up with in a previous short  film that i'd created in unreal engine  which is using sprite animation my idea  was to include really simple sprite  animations into the 3d bedroom to make  it appear as if ghostly versions of  myself were lingering within the room  acting out mundane movements such as  resting on my bed drinking coffee  sitting at my desk playing the guitar  this technique helped to strengthen the  overall narrative as it shows how we  spent a lot of time within the room and  also shows how the music video spans a  large length of time to create this  effect i set up the cameras in the 3d  space where i wanted each animation to  be so that i had reference for the  camera's angle and zoom and then i  recreated these as best as i could in  real life by taking videos of myself  doing the same actions in a real-life  version of the mocked up digital scene i  could then use these as a reference to  trace over in photoshop in photoshop i  imported these videos as video layers  and then scrubbed through the timeline  to find moments of that video that i  would like to trace over and then  created a new layer to trace onto by  repeating this process of tracing onto  new layers at different points of the  video i eventually saw my animation come  to life in the timeline this process  called rotoscope animation takes quite a  lot of time and patience but the outcome  is worth it and once i was happy with  the animation i then selected the layers  i had traced onto and exported them as a  sprite sheet using a photoshop script  developed by jesse freeman i'll put the  link in the description down below  having these set up as a sprite sheet  then would enable me to set them up as  sprite animations within unreal engine  4. and if you'd like a video solely  based on how i do this one effect please  give this video a thumbs up uh because i  can't go into it now we gotta move on  making it personal a music video or any  creative piece in my opinion is pretty  stale if you don't have some personality  in it that others can reflect on or  relate to for this project i was also  heavily influenced by the game life is  strange and it's moments of calm in  particular these were moments in the  game where the player could sit and  reflect on the current state of the  character within the game's story while  the camera panned around their current  surroundings highlighting items of  interest which might inform the player  more about the character or situation  they'd found themselves in throughout  this narrative and i attempted to  recreate this by adding aspects of my  own bedroom into the scene by inserting  polaroids and pictures of me of my  friends and family as well as real  entries from my journal i hope to  inspire some intrigue from the viewer  as they watched my video  and also to make the space feel alive  and lived in to do this i took well-lit  photos of the elements of my room in a  top-down view  and then removed the background in  photoshop making them pngs that could  then be used as a texture on some 3d  models such as an open book  or inside it directly onto a plain 3d  object which is basically just a flat  square in unreal engine and that's how i  created my polaroids  make use of the unreal engine  marketplace  especially when you're working as a solo  creator there's so much that goes into  making a digital video from 3d modeling  to texturing setting up the cameras  sending up the lighting and so so so  much more with this in mind i would  implore you to at least look through the  unreal engine marketplace to see if  there's anything that would help make  your workflow easier you never know when  something that you thought would take  you a long time to do or to learn it's  already available for you on the  marketplace and this will save you time  to focus on the parts of your creation  which are within your expertise for this  project i used  stylized nature packed modular building  sets and interactive stylized low poly  grass to create the outside environment  of my scene beca