as controlled by the  timeline's curve which would play on an  auto loop speed was then multiplied by  delta time from the on tick function  delta time is the time difference  between the previous frame that was  drawn and the current frame and on tick  runs every single frame the output of  this multiplication would update the  current value of artificial time for  that frame i then fed this artificial  time value into the light function  material so that it moved less uniformly  this blueprint held the spotlight which  would have the light function on it  talking blueprints i always just i don't  know if i'm explaining it well  finally i also learned how to manually  control the falloff of my light sources  so i could control how much light  emitted from which source it really  added to the realism of the scene  because it was lit up by multiple weak  light sources by default unreal engines  lights are set up within their details  panel to use inverse squared falloff by  unticking this option you can use  unitless intensity units and then  manipulate the light fall-off exponent  to control exactly  where the light should fall off in  distance around the light source it's a  nifty handy nifty unreal engine  documentation states inverse squared  falloff is physically based distance  fall-off where the attenuation radius is  only clamping the light's contribution  supported by all three light unit types  when disabled only unitless is available  lights with inverse squared falloff  disabled are useful when placing low  intensity fill lights where you don't  want a bright spot near the light  perfect for me so let's talk widgets and  widget animations  widgets are used to display 3d ui  elements within your scene and for this  music video i used widgets to display a  youtube page which was output through a  laptop object and also a text message  display which was output through a phone  object to do this you create a widget  blueprint to set up the ui and any  gameplay functionality for that ui  and then you can reference this within  the output actors blueprint as a widget  component the youtube page was  relatively simple with me incorporating  different sections of a real webpage  such as the tab and sidebars with a  video of the acoustic version of the  song being played  more info on how i got this video into  the widget in the next step the sidebar  incorporated images from my discord  server and also names from my patreons  who without their support i would not be  able to create things like this  i then created blueprint which stored  the laptop static mesh as well as the  widget component layered upon the screen  the phone however was a little bit more  complicated as i had to create a  blueprint which would update the most  recent text message with a string which  is just a series of characters that i  sent from the sequencer  when this happened i also set up widget  animations to create a pop-up effect  to mimic that of a real text message i  also did the same for the three dots  which represent that a person is typing  i created these animations using the  widget blueprints timeline by pressing  the plus animation button down the  bottom left and then keyframing which  element i wanted to change and where  along the allocated animation time frame  these animations then get stored as  variables within the widget blueprints  graph so that i could set up the  functions to trigger the correct  animation at the right time these got  called by the sequencer using sequencer  events more on that later  video image sequence  listen up because this totally changed  my perspective on using in engine video  so i wanted to have a video being played  as a part of the  laptop widget with the youtube page and  i wanted it to show my acoustic version  of the song refill it too as a little  easter egg to those who have watched  both versions  in the past however i have actually  struggled rendering videos through  unreal engine as a video material  because the video playback frame rate  does not stay consistent with the frame  rate of the overall video render this  makes it impossible to know which video  frame will show within the sequence  render  and also displays it at a much slower  speed than it should so this time i  decided to render a video image sequence  instead controlled by the sequencer and  this helped the problem immensely  because it's a video image sequence we  don't have to worry about the frame rate  because it's just a series  of images that it's running through very  quickly this helps extremely with the  timing it's not performant though and i  can't imagine it would be great if  you're making a video game but for a  cinematic it breaks a treat to do this i  created a jpeg sequence from my video  via vlc media player and i will leave a  detailed explanation on how to do this  in the description down below these  images will be stored within a new  folder and in unreal engine i then  created an image media source  which will store your video image  sequence and made this folder of images  the sequence path  overriding the frame rate of its output  to be 24 frames per second to match my  cinematic render this image media source  could then be used as a media track  within the sequencer so i could control  when it was playing and what frames  would play at which point i set the  track to output within the laptop widget  setting the image to be a video material  that got referenced from a media texture  which output whatever was set to play  within a media player it was here that i  set the image media source to play  and yes that was confusing setting up  videos in unreal is extremely  extremely convoluted just remember this  the image media source  is what stores my video sequence of  images  it just says this is the folder of  images  let's make a video out of it the media  player dictates what should be played  so  in my case it was my image media source  the media player 