nhook it  don't have that running  please and then rendering the final  video we're nearly there you nearly made  it well done well done upon rendering i  did end up taking quite a few different  takes and this is perfectly normal and i  would actually say you might want to do  this especially if you have bespoke  things such as particles or animations  as the outcome will be different each  time and it's nice to give yourself a  few different takes to choose from and  work with to speed up the export i left  the audio output format at its default  to no audio this makes sense to me as  the audio with exports as a separate  file anyway so you'd have to work in a  different software to export them  together to export your sequencer you  can do test renders first by ticking use  compression to speed up the process  adjust the compression quality to your  needs with one being the most compressed  and 100 being the best quality you can  also account for warm-up frames to start  your particle effects before your  rendering begins so that your scene  looks at its best step 4  the final step  final touches in premiere pro  using premiere pro or any other video  editing software it's a really nice way  to wrap up the video and also include  the audio this allowed me to choose the  best avi files which are the files that  unreal editor spits out after rendering  and i could use these files to clean up  any transitions and make sure that  everything cut nicely to the beat of the  song when creating the premiere pro  project i had to ensure that it was set  up to match the 24 frames per second  which the render from unreal engine was  you can do that either in the project  settings or by right clicking on an  already imported video file and choosing  modify interpret footage and change  assume this frame rate to 24 frames per  second then you export and boom  you have a little music video bubba  well done you did it  now you can release  wasn't that simple  okay maybe not simp